/*
    On sets, `/` is the INVERSE of the fusing product `*`: it divides a tuple set by a set
    and yields the tuple set of the remaining components.
    Given `S = int * float * string`, `S / string` is `int * float`, and
    `S / (float * string)` is `int`.
    The divisor must correspond to a TRAILING group of components; any other divisor is
    an error.
*/

(int * float * string) / string
